const RULES = `You are working on the Callisto repository (Rust workspace: callisto-model MIT, callisto-manifests, callisto-graph, callisto-cli). You were started in your own git worktree, created from current main. Work only inside it; never edit ${BASE} or any other path.

HARD CONSTRAINTS
- git commands are refused in this sandbox. Do NOT try to commit, branch, push or open PRs: leave your change uncommitted. Use ordinary file tools and cargo.
- Do NOT run \`just ci\` or \`just coverage\` or any whole-workspace test: the coordinator runs the full gate serially. Run only scoped checks for the crate(s) you touch: \`cargo test -p <crate> ...\`, \`cargo clippy -p <crate> --all-targets -- -D warnings\`, \`cargo fmt -p <crate>\`. Run one cargo command at a time. Use \`env -u FORCE_COLOR\` if a test about colors fails.
- Repository rules (AGENTS.md): safe Rust only; TOML edits go through toml_edit and JSON edits preserve key order and indentation, never regex or line edits; file writes go through callisto_model::atomic::atomic_write; user-facing errors derive miette::Diagnostic with a code and a help line; no emojis in docs or code; code comments are one short line and only for a non-obvious why, never history ("previously", "used to"); never cite spec IDs, criterion IDs or section numbers in code, comments, test names or messages; no cargo features.
- Write the failing test FIRST, run it, and confirm it fails for the right reason before writing the fix. Then fix, then run the tests again. Record the failing output in your report. Test adversarial inputs, not only the case being fixed.
- If you cannot reproduce the problem as described, STOP and report what you found instead; do not invent a fix.
- Be precise in your report. Never claim a command passed unless you ran it and saw it pass; list anything you could not run.`

